layout: layouts/tool.njk
toolSlug: fraction-calculator
title: "Fraction Calculator: Add, Subtract, Multiply, Divide"
description: "Add, subtract, multiply and divide fractions and mixed numbers with step-by-step working. Simplify fractions and turn decimals into fractions."
permalink: /tools/fraction-calculator/
h1: "Fraction Calculator"
extraCss: "/tools/fraction-calculator/style.css"
quickAnswer: "To add or subtract fractions, rewrite them over a common denominator and combine the numerators: 1/2 + 1/3 = 3/6 + 2/6 = 5/6. To multiply, multiply tops and bottoms; to divide, multiply by the reciprocal: 3/4 ÷ 1/2 = 3/4 × 2/1 = 6/4 = 3/2 = 1 1/2. Always finish by dividing top and bottom by their greatest common divisor."
formula:
  title: "Fraction Rules"
  expression: "a/b ± c/d = (a·d ± c·b) / (b·d)   ·   a/b × c/d = (a·c) / (b·d)   ·   a/b ÷ c/d = (a·d) / (b·c)"
  explanation: "For addition and subtraction the calculator uses the least common multiple of the denominators, which keeps the numbers small, then adds or subtracts the rewritten numerators. Multiplication multiplies straight across. Division multiplies by the reciprocal of the second fraction, which is impossible if that fraction is zero. The result is reduced by its greatest common divisor (found with Euclid's algorithm) and also shown as a mixed number and a decimal, with any repeating digits in brackets."
  variables:
    - name: "Mixed number"
      description: "A whole number and a fraction, such as 2 1/3. It is converted to an improper fraction first: (2 × 3 + 1)/3 = 7/3."
    - name: "LCM"
      description: "Least common multiple: the smallest number both denominators divide into, used as the common denominator."
    - name: "GCD"
      description: "Greatest common divisor: the largest number that divides both numerator and denominator, used to simplify."
workedExamples:
  - title: "1/2 + 1/3"
    inputs:
      - "1/2 + 1/3"
    steps:
      - "LCM(2, 3) = 6"
      - "1/2 = 3/6 and 1/3 = 2/6"
      - "3/6 + 2/6 = 5/6, already in lowest terms"
    result: "5/6 = 0.8(3)"
    link: "/tools/fraction-calculator/?mode=calc&a=1%2F2&op=add&b=1%2F3#tool"
  - title: "3/4 ÷ 1/2"
    inputs:
      - "3/4 ÷ 1/2"
    steps:
      - "Multiply by the reciprocal: 3/4 × 2/1"
      - "(3 × 2)/(4 × 1) = 6/4"
      - "GCD(6, 4) = 2, so 6/4 = 3/2"
    result: "3/2 = 1 1/2 = 1.5"
    link: "/tools/fraction-calculator/?mode=calc&a=3%2F4&op=divide&b=1%2F2#tool"
  - title: "2 1/3 × 3/7"
    inputs:
      - "2 1/3 × 3/7"
    steps:
      - "2 1/3 = (2 × 3 + 1)/3 = 7/3"
      - "7/3 × 3/7 = 21/21"
      - "GCD(21, 21) = 21, so the answer is 1"
    result: "1"
    link: "/tools/fraction-calculator/?mode=calc&a=2+1%2F3&op=multiply&b=3%2F7#tool"
  - title: "Simplify 6/8 and convert 0.375"
    inputs:
      - "Simplify: 6/8"
      - "Decimal to fraction: 0.375"
    steps:
      - "GCD(6, 8) = 2, so 6/8 = 3/4"
      - "0.375 = 375/1000; GCD(375, 1000) = 125, so 375/1000 = 3/8"
    result: "6/8 = 3/4 and 0.375 = 3/8"
    link: "/tools/fraction-calculator/?mode=dec&dec=0.375#tool"
commonMistakes:
  - title: "Adding denominators"
    description: "1/2 + 1/3 is not 2/5. Denominators say what size the pieces are, so they must match before you add: 3/6 + 2/6 = 5/6."
  - title: "Dividing without flipping the second fraction"
    description: "To divide by a fraction, multiply by its reciprocal (flip the second fraction only). 3/4 ÷ 1/2 = 3/4 × 2/1 = 3/2, not 3/8."
  - title: "Multiplying the whole number of a mixed number separately"
    description: "2 1/3 × 3/7 is not (2 × 3/7) plus (1/3) or 2 × 1/3 × 3/7. Convert 2 1/3 to 7/3 first, then multiply."
  - title: "Stopping before the fraction is simplified"
    description: "6/4 is correct but not finished. Divide top and bottom by their greatest common divisor (2) to get 3/2, or 1 1/2 as a mixed number."
  - title: "Losing the minus sign of a mixed number"
    description: "−1 1/2 means −(1 + 1/2) = −3/2, not −1 + 1/2 = −1/2. Keep the sign outside the whole mixed number."
faqs:
  - q: "How do you add fractions with different denominators?"
    a: "Find a common denominator (the least common multiple of the denominators), rewrite each fraction over it, then add the numerators. 1/2 + 1/3 = 3/6 + 2/6 = 5/6."
  - q: "How do you divide fractions?"
    a: "Keep the first fraction, change ÷ to ×, and flip the second fraction. 3/4 ÷ 1/2 = 3/4 × 2/1 = 6/4 = 3/2."
  - q: "How do you multiply mixed numbers?"
    a: "Convert each mixed number to an improper fraction, multiply the numerators and the denominators, then simplify. 2 1/3 × 3/7 = 7/3 × 3/7 = 21/21 = 1."
  - q: "How do I simplify a fraction?"
    a: "Divide the numerator and denominator by their greatest common divisor. For 6/8 the GCD is 2, so 6/8 = 3/4."
  - q: "How do you turn a decimal into a fraction?"
    a: "Write the digits after the point over 10, 100, 1000 and so on, then simplify. 0.375 = 375/1000, and dividing both by 125 gives 3/8."
  - q: "How do I type a mixed number?"
    a: "Type the whole number, a space, then the fraction: 2 1/3. Negative mixed numbers are written -2 1/3. Whole numbers (5) and decimals (0.75) work too."
  - q: "What does a number in brackets in the decimal mean?"
    a: "The digits in brackets repeat forever. 5/6 = 0.8(3) means 0.8333…, and 1/7 = 0.(142857) means 0.142857142857…."
  - q: "Why can't I divide by zero?"
    a: "Dividing by a fraction means multiplying by its reciprocal, and 0 has no reciprocal. No number times 0 gives a non-zero answer, so division by zero is undefined."
sources:
  - name: "Common Core State Standards for Mathematics: Number and Operations—Fractions"
    url: "https://www.thecorestandards.org/Math/Content/NF/"
  - name: "Euclid's Elements, Book VII, Proposition 2 (greatest common measure)"
