      <h2>Mixed Numbers, Improper Fractions and Decimals</h2>
      <p>An improper fraction has a numerator at least as large as its denominator, such as 7/3. A mixed number writes the same value as a whole number plus a proper fraction: 7 ÷ 3 = 2 remainder 1, so 7/3 = 2 1/3. Calculations are easiest with improper fractions, which is why the calculator converts mixed numbers first and converts back at the end.</p>
      <p>Every fraction is also a decimal. If the simplified denominator has no prime factors other than 2 and 5, the decimal ends (3/8 = 0.375). Otherwise it repeats forever, and the calculator puts the repeating block in brackets: 1/3 = 0.(3), 5/6 = 0.8(3), 1/7 = 0.(142857). Going the other way, only terminating decimals can be typed exactly, so 0.333 becomes 333/1000, not 1/3.</p>

      <h2>How Euclid's Algorithm Finds the GCD</h2>
      <p>To simplify 84/126, divide the larger number by the smaller and keep the remainder: 126 = 1 × 84 + 42, then 84 = 2 × 42 + 0. When the remainder reaches 0, the last divisor (42) is the greatest common divisor, so 84/126 = 2/3. The Simplify mode shows each of these lines, which is handy for checking homework by hand.</p>
      <p>All arithmetic uses exact whole-number maths in your browser, so even very large numerators and denominators are never rounded.</p>
